Goal: Let the user tune a strategy's parameters to find a passing version — WITHOUT the
overfitting trap (the project's core discipline).

Tasks:
[x] experiments/sweep.py: PARAM_GRIDS (primary knob(s) per model, small grids to limit
    multiple testing); sweep_single(prices, family, name, ...) builds each combo (setattr on
    a fresh instance, so it works regardless of __init__ signature) and scores it BOTH
    in-sample (run_backtest_stage) AND walk-forward out-of-sample (run_walk_forward_stage),
    ranked by OOS Sharpe. A config only counts as a real pass when oos_passed. format_sweep
    prints the table + an explicit multiple-testing warning + the OOS verdict. Cross-
    sectional models are rejected (need a basket). grid_for() falls back to a window sweep.
[x] cli.py `sweep <family>/<model> --symbol X` subcommand; interactive menu action
    "4) tune a strategy (parameter sweep)".
[x] Tests: test_sweep.py (5) + CLI sweep case + menu sweep flow.

Why OOS-gated: tuning until something passes on the SAME history is exactly the over-
optimization the project killed in the sector-spread confirmation pass. The sweep judges
each setting on held-out walk-forward folds and shouts the multiple-testing caveat, so a
"pass" means it survived data it was not fit on.

Verification: full suite 544 passed (was 537); ruff clean. LIVE on real SPY (2012+):
`meridian sweep long_term_etf/above_200ma` found window=250 passes OUT-OF-SAMPLE (oos
Sharpe 0.68) and window=100 passes (0.60), while the default window=200 fails — "2 of 4
passed", with the overfitting warning. A real tuning win surfaced honestly.

Usage:
  python -m meridian sweep long_term_etf/above_200ma --symbol SPY   # tune, OOS-validated
  (or menu -> 4) tune a strategy)

--- SESSION 020b (confirmation) ---
Date: 2026-06-26
Confirmed the sweep's window=250 winner through the FULL pipeline (backtest -> walk_forward
-> fixed 2010-2019/2020-2022 OOS holdout) on full SPY history (1993-2026). VERDICT: does
NOT hold up. On the fixed OOS holdout, window=250 scores Sharpe 0.38 (< 0.5 bar -> FAIL),
while the default window=200 PASSES the holdout (0.55). The ranking flipped vs the sweep
(which ran on SPY 2012+ walk-forward and ranked 250 first at 0.68) — a date-window/fold
artifact, i.e. exactly the multiple-testing risk the sweep warns about. Both windows also
fail the full-history backtest/walk-forward gates (>20% drawdown across 2008/2020 ->
retired). Lesson reaffirmed: a walk-forward tuning win is a hypothesis; the independent
fixed holdout is the judge — here it rejected window=250. (No code change; analysis only.)

